          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining sparse and dense search to improve retrieval accuracy in RAG and search systems.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - pattern/Hybrid-Search\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Two sources now available; pattern validates in production systems but general principle could use additional corroboration.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets, often via reciprocal rank fusion (RRF), to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\n### Why Hybrid Works\\n\\nThe approach consistently outperforms either method alone on queries that mix:\\n\\n- Rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs, reference numbers)\\n- Natural language intent and semantic meaning\\n\\nBy combining both signals, hybrid retrieval avoids the failure modes of each method alone:\\n- Pure lexical search misses semantic paraphrases and synonyms\\n- Pure vector search may fail on exact-match queries with rare or unique terms\\n\\n### Production Adoption\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems, validating its effectiveness and reliability at scale.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Internal source \\u2014 hybrid retrieval mechanisms and production patterns.\\n\u0022}"
        }
